  <name>Live analysis lab</name>
  <author>Z. Cliffe Schreuders</author>
  <description>
    # Introduction
    A server on your network has been compromised. The intruder has planted a backdoor, is running processes they would rather you did not see, and has loaded a rootkit that lies to the usual command-line tools (ps, ss and the like) so the live system hides its own compromise. Your task is to work out what is really running on that machine and to recover the evidence the attacker tried to conceal.

    You will do this with memory forensics. Memory holds what the disk and the live tools do not: the full command line of every process, the environment a daemon was started with, a process whose executable has been deleted, the in-memory history of a shell that was told not to save it. A RAM capture is also beyond the reach of a rootkit that hooks the running kernel's tools.

    The lab has two halves. First you capture memory from the compromised server yourself, from inside the running system, and try to analyse it. There you meet the main problem of live acquisition: a capture taken while the kernel keeps running is not one consistent moment, so the plugins that walk the kernel's own lists give unreliable results. Then you analyse an atomic capture of the same system, taken with the virtual machine paused from the hypervisor so every structure is consistent, and recover the artefacts cleanly with Volatility 3. Along the way you will see why atomic acquisition matters, and which techniques (string search, pool scanning) still work when it is not available.

    This is a Hackerbot lab. Work through the lab sheet, then interact with Hackerbot to complete the challenges.
  </description>

